-- Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One, three robots (printed 180-190): Thundercaster NG-V58, Viking NG-V41, Volcano NG-V39
-- 3 vehicle rows, with 48 locations and 12 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~291-northern-gun-1-robots-g.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('ng-v58-thundercaster', 'Thundercaster Combat Robot', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Two: a pilot, who drives and fires the arm weapons, and a gunner (or a passenger in that seat), who usually works the Thunder Cannon', 'None beyond the gunner''s seat, which a passenger may fill', 'Running 55 mph (88 km) maximum. Leaping 7 ft (2.1 m) high and 14 ft (4.3 m) across. Drops to its hands and knees in one melee action and rises in one more, so a loader can reach the cannon', 'Not possible', 'Cannot swim; walks lake and sea floors at 20 mph (32 km); maximum depth 2,000 ft (610 m)', 'Height 17 ft (5.2 m) head to toe, though the Thunder Cannon can be raised above that; width 14 ft (4.3 m); length 8 ft 6 in (2.6 m)', '10.5', 400, 1, NULL, 14000000, 'Nuclear 23 million credits, Solid Oxide Fuel Cell 17 million, electric battery 14 million; the lowest is stored. Every price includes a full ammunition load for all weapons. Demand is strong: some stores sell it 20-30% over list. Fair availability.', 'Model Type: NG-V58 Thundercaster. Nicknames: Walking Thunder, Michigan Wolverine and Little Wolverine. Class: Infantry Assault Robot. A compact, thickly armored and heavily gunned robot from Jason M. Volinsky, David Thompson and Randy P. Belanger, who modeled it on the wolverine, a small animal that holds its ground against much bigger predators; marketing overruled the animal name in favor of Thundercaster. On sale only since summer 109 P.A., it is NG''s newest robot, aimed at mercenaries and independent fighters, and its odd main gun has made it a strong seller. The main gun is an air-pressure cannon on the right shoulder that booms when it fires and will throw its own artillery shells or nearly any object of the right size (see its weapon entry), so a crew in the wilds never runs short of ammunition and can load wood, stone, iron, silver, salt or bone against a monster vulnerable to one. The right arm is a multi-barrel rail gun that bends only above the biceps, so the gun swings 90 degrees up and down while the hand can still lift and carry; a belt from a drum on the back feeds it. The introduction calls this gun an NG-404 gatling-style rail gun; the weapon entry names it the NG-R58, with figures of its own, and the entry is followed here. The left forearm carries a twin laser, and the big shoulders store spare cannon shells where a kneeling robot''s loader can reach them. The head is the sensor and radio array; pilot and gunner ride in the chest behind a plexiglass window that sliding M.D.C. plates cover in combat at a button or voice command, the pilot then driving by camera and instruments; a window takes a Called Shot at -3, and once glass and plate are gone the Reinforced Pilot Compartment is open to attack. Robot P.S. 35. Cargo: a small personal stowage space that fits a pistol, a rifle, light body armor, a first aid kit, two canteens and two weeks of rations, plus a built-in water cooler of 2 gallons (7.6 liters). Power: nuclear, 20 year life; fuel cell and battery models are also offered. Hand to hand per Light Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.353) at Robotic P.S. 35. Sensors: the robot standard plus enhanced radar tracking 90 targets to 100 miles (160 km) with a large IFF library ranking threats, giving +1 initiative and +1 strike with the rail gun and laser but not the Thunder Cannon; a scrambled wideband and directional radio relaying audio and video 500 miles (800 km), a third of that in mountains, underground or storms; a microphone hearing a whisper at 400 ft (122 m) with 100 hours of audio storage; five cameras (head, each arm, cannon and belly) storing 8,000 hours between them, sent live or later to the crew and to teammates'' helmet HUDs, through which the pilot drives and fights; and a translator for the Americas'' nine known languages with room for 12 more, following up to three voices and two languages, 98.7% accurate with a three second delay for one speaker, 78% with six seconds for three.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.180-184'),
  ('ng-v41-viking', 'Viking Combat Robot', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Two: a pilot, who drives and works the mouth grenade launcher and the claws, and a gunner on the main cannon and the missile launchers', 'Two; four when cramped', 'Running 45 mph (72 km). Leaping is limited: only with a running start, 6 ft (1.8 m) high and 12 ft (3.6 m) across', 'Not possible', 'Not designed for water; walks lake and sea floors at 20 mph (32 km); maximum depth 2,500 ft (762 m)', 'Height 29 ft (8.8 m); width 15 ft (4.6 m); length 14 ft (4.3 m)', '54', 745, 1, NULL, 52000000, 'Nuclear 64 million credits, Solid Oxide Fuel Cell 56 million, electric battery 52 million; the lowest is stored, though NG does not recommend either alternative. Swapping one shoulder launcher for the other missile type costs 70,000 credits at any competent Operator.', 'Model Type: NG-V41 Viking. Nicknames: Rabbit, Big Bunny and Killer Bunny. Class: Infantry Assault and Tactical Support Robot. A huge, slow, armor-heavy robot built first for troop support and mobile artillery and also good at construction and demolition, from a team led by Ian Small, Daniel Ogle, Peter Smith and Ryan McDaniel under NG''s ''upward mobility'' push: its guns breach fortifications and knock down old buildings, and its strength and sword-length Vibro-Claws take on dinosaurs and giant monsters. Towns, city-states and bases use it as a walking defensive bunker. Its armor is among the heaviest NG has ever sold, enough to stand off one to three Glitter Boys, at the price of a massive venting and cooling system and a speed NG could not raise without thinning it. The head is the sensor and radio array, with two ordinary and two infrared searchlights, cameras and four radar dishes that were meant as the horns of a Viking helmet but look like rabbit ears, hence the nicknames, which crews use fondly. Pilot and gunner sit in the chest with two passenger seats; it serves an expedition only as the defensive anchor of a larger convoy. Every seat reclines for sleep, and there is a small cab with two bunks, lockers for two sets of light power armor or body armor, an ion-vaporizing toilet, a wash basin, a tiny shower, and 200 gallons (757 liters) of water with purification and filtering. Robot P.S. 45. Cargo: the sleeping cab, two footlockers, a weapon locker holding 4 pistols, 4 rifles, 8 flares, 4 smoke grenades, 4 canteens, a first aid kit and 2 survival kits, room under each of the four seats for a backpack and some personal effects, and two small airliner-style compartments for the passenger seats. Power: nuclear, 15 year life; fuel cell and battery models exist, though NG advises against them. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352) at Robotic P.S. 45, except: restrained punch 1D6 M.D.; punch 3D6 M.D.; power punch 1D6x10 M.D., counting as two attacks; claw strike 4D6 M.D.; Vibro-Blade claw strike 6D6+4 M.D.; crush, pry or tear 4D6 M.D.; dig with the hands 2D6 M.D., +2D6 with the claws out; kick 5D8 M.D.; no leap kick; stomp 2D4 M.D., only against targets 8 ft (2.4 m) tall or less. Sensors: the robot standard plus a scrambled wideband and directional radio relaying audio and video 500 miles (800 km), a third of that in mountains, underground or storms; a microphone hearing a whisper at 400 ft (122 m) with 100 hours of audio storage; loudspeakers; six cameras (two head, two arm, one chest, one back) storing 8,000 hours between them, sent live or later to the crew and to teammates'' helmet HUDs, through which the pilot drives and fights; a translator for the Americas'' nine known languages with room for 12 more, following up to three voices and two languages, 98.7% accurate with a three second delay for one speaker, 78% with six seconds for three; and four head searchlights, the two large ones on top ordinary and the two eye-like ones infrared.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.184-187'),
  ('ng-v39-volcano', 'Volcano Combat Robot', 'rifts', 'robot', 'One pilot, with a seat for a crewman, gunner or passenger', 'One, in the second seat, if it is not a gunner''s', 'Running 60 mph (96 km). Leaping only with a running start, 10 ft (3 m) high and 16 ft (4.9 m) across', 'Not possible', 'Not designed for water; walks the bottom at 25 mph (40 km); maximum depth 1,000 ft (305 m). The Heatwave Emitter and flamethrower do not work underwater; the plasma ejector does, at 400 ft (122 m) range', 'Height 24 ft (7.3 m); width 15 ft (4.6 m); length 12 ft (3.6 m)', '23', 400, 1, NULL, 31000000, 'Nuclear 38 million credits, Solid Oxide Fuel Cell 31 million; the lowest is stored. No electric battery version: the robot cannot run on one.', 'Model Type: NG-V39. Class: Infantry Assault Robot. A fire-weapon robot from NG''s Morpheus group under Dan Deluca, Rosalena Butka, Amanda Butka and Scott Gibbons, who saw no NG machine filling that role; most animals, monsters, and many supernatural beings and creatures of magic fear flame, and NG hopes it will clear out and burn Xiticix nests and hives. It torches its surroundings, flushes troops from bunkers and APCs, and sells to buyers bound for the Canadian tundra and the icy American north to face fire-vulnerable demons, monsters and aliens. Its main gun is a long-range flamethrower above the right shoulder, fed from a big drum on the back; the right forearm holds the experimental Heatwave Emitter, a blocky box with three slots that look made for saws but instead project a heat ray; the left forearm carries a plasma ejector. Built to range far over rough ground, it seats the pilot and a gunner or crewman in the chest, with reclining seats for sleep, an ion-vaporizing toilet, a basin fed by a 100 gallon (378.5 liter) tank, plus another 100 gallons of drinking water. Best on raids, sabotage and monster-nest burning; one or a few round out most robot squads and mercenary companies. Robot P.S. 40. Cargo: a small locker for a rifle, a pistol, a survival kit and some personal effects. Power: nuclear, 15 year life; Solid Oxide Fuel Cell version available; no electric battery version. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352) at Robotic P.S. 40, except: restrained punch 1D4 M.D.; full strength punch 2D6 M.D.; power punch 4D6 M.D., counting as two attacks; kick 3D8 M.D.; no leap kick; stomp 1D6 M.D., only against targets 6 ft (1.8 m) tall or less. Sensors: the book gives it the Viking''s suite: the robot standard plus a scrambled wideband and directional radio relaying audio and video 500 miles (800 km), a third of that in mountains, underground or storms; a microphone hearing a whisper at 400 ft (122 m) with 100 hours of audio storage; cameras storing 8,000 hours between them, sent live or later to the crew and to teammates'' helmet HUDs; a translator for the Americas'' nine known languages with room for 12 more; and head searchlights, two ordinary and two infrared.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.187-190');

